    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're touching on something fascinating, linking logistical failure to a kind of environmental *spirit*—and while I don't believe in actual poltergeists, I can certainly explain the human and systemic reasons why these \"goblins\" congregate in high-stress zones. The chaos you perceive is often a function of extreme pressure meeting imperfect systems.\n\nBorder crossings and busy airports are perfect breeding grounds for minor disasters because they are points of maximum friction. Think of it like a massive, poorly managed traffic jam where every single vehicle (or document) is moving at maximum speed while simultaneously being scrutinized by a very tired gatekeeper.\n\nThe \"mischief\" is amplified because of the sheer volume and the high stakes. When hundreds of people are rushing through a bottleneck, the minor human errors—the misplaced folder, the tired clerk overlooking a stamp—become magnified into major logistical threats. It’s not a spirit; it’s simply the physics of extreme congestion interacting with human fallibility.\n\nThe environment itself is designed for high throughput, not for meticulous care, which is exactly where those tiny, twitchy disruptions thrive. It’s a pressure cooker where a single, overlooked detail can cause a cascade failure.",
